# Rune Trademark Policy

Version 1.0, effective November 12, 2026

Rune is free software. You may use, study, modify and share its code under the Mozilla Public License 2.0, and its assets under the Rune Asset License. This policy covers what those licenses do not: the Rune name and logo. Its purpose is simple. Anything that carries the Rune name should be what users expect Rune to be, and nobody should be misled about who made or distributes it.

## 1. The Rune Marks

This policy covers the following (the "Rune Marks"):

- the names "Rune" and "Rune Player", when used for music software or related services;
- the Rune logo and application icon, including the icon files under `assets/icons/`;
- any name or logo that is confusingly similar to the above.

The Rune Marks are held by the Rune Development Team (the "Project"). This policy applies whether or not a Rune Mark is registered.

## 2. How This Policy Relates to the Licenses

The licenses grant copyright permissions. They do not grant any rights in the Rune Marks: see Section 2.3 of the Mozilla Public License 2.0 and Section 2(b)(2) of the Rune Asset License (`assets/LICENSE`).

The Rune Asset License lets you copy and adapt the logo artwork. This policy decides whether you may use the logo, or anything derived from it, to identify software or a service. When you use the Rune Marks, you must follow both.

## 3. Official Channels

A build is official only if the Project, or a distributor acting under a written agreement with the Project, publishes it through one of these channels:

- GitHub Releases and CI builds in the official repository: https://github.com/Losses/rune
- Steam: https://store.steampowered.com/app/3343500/Rune/
- Microsoft Store: https://apps.microsoft.com/detail/9N52TW1F5348
- Apple App Store, published by an authorized distributor: https://apps.apple.com/us/app/rune-player/id6738841909

The Project adds new channels to this list as they open. Only builds from these channels may be called "official".

## 4. Uses That Need No Permission

### 4.1 Talking about Rune

You may use the Rune name and logo to refer to Rune truthfully, for example in news coverage, articles, reviews, tutorials, videos, talks, academic work and social media posts, and in statements such as "works with Rune" or "a plugin for Rune". You do not need to ask first. Do not suggest that the Project endorses, sponsors or is affiliated with you or your work unless it does.

### 4.2 Sharing official builds

You may share unmodified official builds under the Rune name, as long as you do not charge for them.

### 4.3 Community packages

You may build Rune and distribute it under the Rune Marks through a package repository or app catalog, such as Flathub, the AUR, a Linux distribution's repository, Homebrew, Scoop, Nix or F-Droid, as long as all of the following are true:

1. **Built from official source.** The build comes from a release tag or a commit in the official repository.
2. **Limited changes.** Your changes are limited to:
   - packaging and integration with the target system, such as build options, file locations, desktop integration, sandbox permissions and use of system libraries;
   - porting to another platform or architecture;
   - bug fixes and security fixes, which we encourage you to send upstream;
   - turning on or off features that already exist in Rune;
   - translations.
3. **Changes are public.** Your changes are published where users can find them, for example as patch files in your package source.
4. **No charge.** You do not charge for the package, and you have not added paid features or advertising to it. A link inviting voluntary donations to you as the package maintainer is fine, as long as nothing in the package depends on it.
5. **Marked as unofficial.** The package description states that the package is community-maintained, that it is not an official Rune build, and who maintains it.
6. **No official listing there.** The Project has no official listing in that repository or catalog.

If the Project later opens an official listing in a repository or catalog where your package exists, it will tell you. You will then have 90 days to rename your package, withdraw it, or work with the Project to hand over the listing.

The Project may also ask a specific package to revert a change, or to stop using the Rune Marks, if that change causes problems for users. If this happens, please comply within a reasonable time.

How support works for community packages is described in [SUPPORT.md](SUPPORT.md).

## 5. Uses That Need Permission

You need written permission from the Project to:

- charge for any software or service that carries the Rune Marks, or offer it with in-app purchases or advertising;
- publish a listing under the Rune Marks in a store or catalog where an official listing exists;
- use the Rune Marks as or within the name of your own product, company or domain name, except for community package names allowed under Section 4.3, such as a package named `rune`;
- make merchandise carrying the Rune Marks for sale;
- use the Rune Marks in any other way not covered by Section 4.

## 6. Modified Versions

If your changes go beyond Section 4.3, your software is a modified version. Examples include adding features, adding telemetry, advertising or third-party services, changing default servers or update channels, and changing the user interface.

For a modified version, you must:

- give it a name and an icon that are not confusingly similar to the Rune Marks;
- not present it as Rune or as an official build.

You may say truthfully that it is "based on Rune" or "a fork of Rune", as long as you also state that it is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Project.

## 7. Earlier Versions

This policy applies to the Rune Marks in connection with every version of Rune, including versions released before this policy was published. Neither the Mozilla Public License 2.0 nor the Rune Asset License has ever granted rights in the Rune Marks, so this policy does not withdraw any right those licenses granted.

The copyright licenses that came with earlier versions remain in effect for those versions. This policy does not change them.

If you were already using the Rune Marks on the effective date of this policy in a way that does not meet it, you have 90 days from that date to bring your use into line.

## 8. Changes to This Policy

The Project may update this policy. Each version carries a version number and an effective date, and changes apply from the date they are published.

## 9. Questions and Reports

To request permission, to ask whether a use is allowed, or to report misuse of the Rune Marks, open an issue at https://github.com/Losses/rune/issues.